B.Hd=s(["A leather pocketbook made from Burke's skin","A letter signed in Burke's blood","A moulded model of Burke's head","Burke's skeleton"],t.s)
B.N7=new A.bG("scholar_q3",B.co,"Which of the following can be found at the Surgeons' Hall Museum?","Select all that apply.",B.Hd,B.fI,"The Surgeons' Hall Museum holds the pocketbook, the blood-signed letter, and the head model. Burke's skeleton is at the University of Edinburgh anatomy section, as his body still belongs to the university.",null,null,null,null,null,null)
B.Ir=s(["A ban on Presbyterian worship","A Book of Common Prayer","A tax on church land","The National Covenant"],t.s)
B.Nj=new A.bG("scholar_q4",B.aq,"What did King Charles I impose on Scotland's churches in 1637?",null,B.Ir,1,"Charles I introduced a Book of Common Prayer to align Scotland's Kirk with the Church of England, causing widespread outrage among Presbyterians.",null,null,null,null,null,null)
B.Ge=s(["She was a Covenanter leader","She was Charles I's Catholic wife, fuelling suspicion his reforms were pro-Catholic",u.Z],t.s)
B.Ln={"She was a Covenanter leader":0,[u.Z]:1}
B.G1=new A.hh("Who Led the Covenanters?","The Covenanters had no single leader but were driven by Presbyterian ministers and nobles who opposed the Crown's interference in Scottish worship. Key figures included Archibald Campbell, Marquess of Argyll, and Richard Cameron, whose followers became known as the Cameronians.")
B.G0=new A.hh("Who Signed the National Covenant?","The National Covenant was signed by thousands of ordinary Scots at Greyfriars Kirk in 1638, not by any royal figure. Copies were sent across Scotland for more signatures. It was a grassroots act of defiance against the Crown, not a royal decree. The original document is still on display at the Greyfriars Museum.")
B.KB=new A.bw(B.Ln,[B.G1,B.G0],t.EM)
B.Ni=new A.bG("scholar_q5",B.aq,"Why was Henrietta Maria significant to the Covenanters' conflict?",null,B.Ge,1,"Charles I's marriage to Henrietta Maria, a Roman Catholic French princess, deepened Scottish fears that his church reforms were designed to move Scotland toward Catholicism.",null,null,null,null,null,B.KB)
B.It=s(["National Museum of Scotland","Edinburgh Castle","Greyfriars Museum","British Library"],t.s)
B.Ns=new A.bG("scholar_q6",B.aq,"Where can the original National Covenant document be seen today?",null,B.It,2,"The original National Covenant document is on display within the Greyfriars Museum, right where it was signed in 1638.",null,null,null,null,null,null)
B.Is=s(["Battle of Dunbar","Battle of Bothwell Bridge","Battle of Killiecrankie","Battle of Prestonpans"],t.s)
B.Lc={"Battle of Dunbar":0,"Battle of Killiecrankie":1,"Battle of Prestonpans":2}
B.FZ=new A.hh("Battle of Dunbar (1650)","Fought between Oliver Cromwell's English forces and the Scottish Covenanter army. Despite being outnumbered, Cromwell achieved a decisive victory, leading to the English occupation of southern Scotland. This was a major blow to Scottish independence during the Wars of the Three Kingdoms.")
B.G_=new A.hh("Battle of Killiecrankie (1689)","A dramatic Highland battle where Jacobite forces, loyal to the exiled King James VII, defeated a government army in a fierce charge through the Pass of Killiecrankie. Despite their victory, the Jacobite commander Viscount Dundee was killed, and the rising soon lost momentum.")
B.G2=new A.hh("Battle of Prestonpans (1745)","The first major battle of the 1745 Jacobite Rising, fought just east of Edinburgh. Bonnie Prince Charlie's Highland army routed the government forces in less than fifteen minutes, in one of the most stunning victories in Scottish military history.")
B.KN=new A.bw(B.Lc,[B.FZ,B.G_,B.G2],t.EM)
B.Np=new A.bG("scholar_q7",B.aq,"What battle in 1679 led to 1,200 Covenanters being imprisoned at Greyfriars?",null,B.Is,1,"The Covenanters experienced a crushing defeat at the Battle of Bothwell Bridge in 1679. Around 1,200 captured Covenanters were marched to Edinburgh and imprisoned beside Greyfriars Kirkyard.",null,null,null,null,null,B.KN)
B.HM=s(["He served as Lord Advocate",u.n,u.y,"He signed the National Covenant",u.C],t.s)
B.Gb=s([0,1,2,4],t.t)
B.Lr={"He served as Lord Advocate":0,[u.n]:1,[u.y]:2,[u.C]:3}
B.G4=new A.hh("Lord Advocate","The Lord Advocate was Scotland's chief legal officer, responsible for prosecuting crimes on behalf of the Crown. McKenzie used this powerful position to lead the relentless persecution of the Covenanters, overseeing trials that sent hundreds to imprisonment, transportation, or death.")
B.G3=new A.hh("The Scottish Witch Trials","McKenzie gained early notoriety for his involvement in the Scottish Witch Trials, particularly his defence of an alleged witch named Maevia. Unusually for the time, he argued against the charges, demonstrating his sharp legal mind. Scotland saw over 3,800 people accused of witchcraft between the 16th and 18th centuries.")
B.G6=new A.hh("Aretina (1660)","McKenzie wrote 'Aretina', a romantic work often considered the first Scottish novel. It was published in 1660 and showed a very different side to the man who would later become infamous for his cruelty. He was also a prolific essayist and legal writer.")
B.G5=new A.hh("The Advocates Library","McKenzie played a key role in establishing the Advocates Library in 1689, which served as Scotland's leading legal library. It later evolved into the National Library of Scotland, one of the country's most important cultural institutions, housing millions of items.")
B.KQ=new A.bw(B.Lr,[B.G4,B.G3,B.G6,B.G5],t.EM)
B.Nf=new A.bG("scholar_q8",B.co,"Which of the following are true about George McKenzie?","Select all that apply.",B.HM,B.Gb,"McKenzie served as Lord Advocate, was involved in the Witch Trials (defending the alleged witch Maevia), wrote 'Aretina' (considered the first Scottish novel), and helped establish the Advocates Library. He did not sign the National Covenant.",null,null,null,null,null,B.KQ)
B.Ip=s(["It is the largest tomb in Edinburgh","It is one of the finest examples of 17th century cemetery architecture","It was designed by a famous French architect","It contains stained glass windows from the original Greyfriars Kirk"],t.s)
B.N6=new A.bG("scholar_q9",B.aq,"Why is the Black Mausoleum considered architecturally significant?",null,B.Ip,1,"The Black Mausoleum is considered one of the finest examples of 17th century cemetery architecture, though it is better known today as one of the most haunted sites in the United Kingdom.",null,null,null,null,null,null)
B.Hz=s(['"Bluidy Mackingie, come oot if ye dar, lift the sneck and draw the bar"','"McKenzie, McKenzie, rise from yer bed, show us yer face and strike us all dead"','"Bloody George, we call yer name, leave yer tomb and play our game"','"Rise up McKenzie, cold and grey, haunt us now and make us pay"'],t.s)
B.N8=new A.bG("scholar_q10",B.aq,"What did the old Heriot's school rhyme dare people to say at McKenzie's tomb?",null,B.Hz,0,'The traditional Heriot\'s school rhyme goes: "Bluidy Mackingie, come oot if ye dar, lift the sneck and draw the bar." Some visitors still dare to recite it at his tomb.',null,null,null,null,null,null)
B.N5=new A.bG("scholar_q11",B.co,u._,"Select all that apply.",B.ns,B.fI,"The National Covenant was signed at Greyfriars in 1638, pledging to defend the Scottish Kirk's independence in response to Charles I's reforms. McKenzie was not involved.",null,null,null,null,null,null)
B.IO=s(["Charles I imposes the Book of Common Prayer on Scotland","The National Covenant is signed at Greyfriars in protest","Covenanters are defeated at the Battle of Bothwell Bridge","1,200 prisoners are marched to Greyfriars and imprisoned"],t.s)
B.Ng=new A.bG("scholar_q12",B.k0,u.I,null,B.bb,null,"Charles I's imposition of the Book of Common Prayer in 1637 sparked the National Covenant in 1638, leading to civil war, the defeat at Bothwell Bridge in 1679, and the imprisonment of 1,200 Covenanters at Greyfriars.",null,null,null,B.IO,B.jm,null)
B.I0=s(["Henrietta Maria","The National Covenant","Battle of Bothwell Bridge","Margaret Docherty"],t.s)
B.Nk=new A.bG("scholar_q13",B.xy,"ODD ONE OUT: Which one does NOT belong?",null,B.I0,3,"Margaret Docherty was Burke and Hare's last victim. The other three are all part of the Covenanters' story.",null,null,null,null,null,null)
B.Hv=s(["1832","1679","1638","1828"],t.s)
B.Hx=s(["Burke and Hare murders","The Anatomy Act is passed","Covenanters imprisoned after Battle of Bothwell Bridge","The National Covenant is signed at Greyfriars"],t.s)
B.KO=new A.cc([0,1,1,2,2,3,3,0],t.KL)
B.Nc=new A.bG("scholar_q14",B.k_,"Match each date to the correct event:",null,B.bb,null,"1638: National Covenant signed. 1679: Covenanters imprisoned after Bothwell Bridge. 1828: Burke and Hare committed their murders. 1832: The Anatomy Act was passed.",B.Hv,B.Hx,B.KO,null,null,null)
